1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,850
Hello I'm Russell and welcome
to the Articulate Journal

2
00:00:04,170 --> 00:00:06,860
where I'm exploring what
it takes to build an

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,820
AI-native architecture from first
principles through the design and

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:15,280
implementation of a reference architecture
for Architectural Intelligence.

5
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:18,800
This isn't a tutorial
series or a product demo,

6
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:22,570
it's a public engineering journal
documenting the architectural decisions,

7
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:26,310
experiments, and lessons learned
as the architecture evolves.

8
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:29,880
In the previous episode
I explored the idea of

9
00:00:30,010 --> 00:00:33,610
treating architecture as knowledge.
That information exists across different

10
00:00:33,930 --> 00:00:38,170
architectural domains. The business
capability may be supported by

11
00:00:38,210 --> 00:00:43,860
application components. Those components
may depend on data, integrations,

12
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:48,840
and infrastructure. Decisions
explain why parts of the architecture

13
00:00:48,970 --> 00:00:50,710
exist in their current form.

14
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:56,360
Principles influence those decisions.
System characteristics provide constraints

15
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:58,840
that shape the resulting design.

16
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:02,810
The Knowledge Model gives
Articulate a way to represent

17
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:06,660
this information and importantly
the relationships between it, but

18
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:09,990
representing knowledge
creates another question.

19
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:12,990
How should AI reason with that knowledge?

20
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:18,160
This is where the problem
becomes more interesting than

21
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:23,040
simply retrieving information.
Imagine asking Articulate a question.

22
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,160
What would be affected if
we replaced our current

23
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:32,220
messaging platform? Finding information
about the messaging platform is

24
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:35,720
only the beginning. To provide
a useful architectural answer,

25
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:39,090
the system may need to identify
which application components

26
00:01:39,170 --> 00:01:42,980
depend on it. It may
then need to understand

27
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:45,970
which business capabilities
those components support.

28
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:51,370
There may be architectural
decisions that explain why the

29
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:53,900
messaging platform was
originally selected.

30
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:57,540
Those decisions may have been
influenced by particular system

31
00:01:57,580 --> 00:02:03,370
characteristics or architectural
principles. A proposed replacement may

32
00:02:03,410 --> 00:02:06,720
satisfy some of those constraints
while conflicting with others.

33
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:10,060
The answer therefore does
not exist as a single

34
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:12,680
piece of information
waiting to be retrieved.

35
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:16,360
It has to be constructed
from the knowledge available.

36
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:19,460
This creates an important distinction.

37
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:24,200
Retrieval gathers relevant knowledge.
Reasoning determines what that knowledge

38
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:26,040
means in the context of the question.

39
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:30,440
This distinction is important
when thinking about how AI

40
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:32,900
systems interact with
architectural knowledge.

41
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:40,270
Retrieval-Augmented Generation, or
RAG, has become a common architectural

42
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:44,130
pattern for giving language models
access to information beyond

43
00:02:44,170 --> 00:02:49,040
their training data. A typical
implementation takes documents, divides

44
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:53,640
them into chunks, creates vector
embeddings, and retrieves chunks

45
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:57,680
that are semantically similar
to the user's question.

46
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:01,690
This is useful. Articulate
may use semantic search for

47
00:03:01,730 --> 00:03:05,960
exactly this reason. But
architectural knowledge is not simply

48
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:07,740
a collection of text fragments.

49
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:12,900
Consider the following question.
Which business capabilities would be

50
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:16,800
affected if this infrastructure
component failed?

51
00:03:16,890 --> 00:03:21,100
The answer is not necessarily
contained within a semantically

52
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:25,090
similar paragraph. The system
may need to follow relationships.

53
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:29,840
An infrastructure component
hosts an application component.

54
00:03:29,890 --> 00:03:33,630
That application component
supports a business capability.

55
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:37,860
The relationship between those
concepts itself is part of

56
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:42,740
the knowledge. Semantic similarity
can help us find relevant

57
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:45,850
information, but it
cannot be the only way we

58
00:03:45,890 --> 00:03:49,820
navigate architectural knowledge.
This is one of the reasons,

59
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:54,060
such as approaches like RAG,
GraphRAG are interesting.

60
00:03:54,570 --> 00:03:58,640
Rather than treating information
only as isolated chunks of

61
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:02,050
text, a graph allows relationships
between information to become

62
00:04:02,090 --> 00:04:03,580
part of the retrieval process.

63
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:08,040
This aligns naturally with the
Knowledge Model introduced in

64
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:13,300
the previous episode. If
Articulate knows that, a business

65
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:17,620
capability is supported by
an application component.

66
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:20,640
An application component
depends on an integration.

67
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:23,770
That integration uses
a messaging platform.

68
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:28,220
The messaging platform is posted
on particular infrastructure.

69
00:04:28,370 --> 00:04:31,520
Then a question about infrastructure
can be explored through

70
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:35,560
those relationships. The important
shift is that retrieval is

71
00:04:35,630 --> 00:04:38,400
no longer only about finding
information that looks similar

72
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:42,050
to the question. It can
also be about navigating

73
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:44,390
the structure of what is known.

74
00:04:44,490 --> 00:04:48,090
However, graph traversal
is not the answer to every

75
00:04:48,130 --> 00:04:52,000
question either. Some questions
are semantic, some are relational,

76
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:56,420
some are structured. Others
may require several of these

77
00:04:56,500 --> 00:05:01,140
approaches together. The important
architectural principle, therefore, is not

78
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:05,300
that Articulate should use
vector search, knowledge graph or

79
00:05:05,350 --> 00:05:09,840
structured queries. It is that
different questions require different

80
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:13,840
ways of retrieving knowledge.
This leads to a broader

81
00:05:14,010 --> 00:05:18,190
question. Before Articulate can
decide how to retrieve information,

82
00:05:18,270 --> 00:05:21,610
it needs to understand
what kind of question it

83
00:05:21,650 --> 00:05:25,460
is trying to answer.
Consider these questions.

84
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:27,490
What depends on this component?

85
00:05:27,650 --> 00:05:30,080
Why did we choose this technology?

86
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:34,060
Does this proposal conflict
with an architectural principle?

87
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:37,840
What changed between these
two architectural states?

88
00:05:37,930 --> 00:05:40,070
What evidence supports this claim?

89
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:44,320
Each question requires something
different from the system.

90
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:49,390
The first requires understanding
relationships and dependencies.

91
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:53,210
The second requires reconstructing
the context around a decision.

92
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:58,010
The third requires evaluating a
proposal against existing constraints.

93
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:01,700
The fourth requires reasoning across time.

94
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:05,390
The fifth requires tracing knowledge
back to supporting evidence.

95
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:09,990
Calling all of this simply
agent reasoning hides an

96
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:12,850
important part of the architecture.

97
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,920
The system needs different
reasoning capabilities.

98
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:22,760
The first is relational reasoning
and explores how architectural

99
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:27,040
concepts are connected. It
allows questions such as what

100
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:30,400
depends on this? What does this support?

101
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,060
Which parts of the architecture
are connected to this

102
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:38,890
decision? This form of reasoning
naturally relies heavily on

103
00:06:38,970 --> 00:06:42,390
the relationships captured with
inside the Knowledge Model.

104
00:06:42,890 --> 00:06:48,820
Architecture contains constraints,
principles, system characteristics,

105
00:06:48,910 --> 00:06:54,070
policies, and previous decisions,
all influence what constitutes as

106
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:59,720
an appropriate design. Constraint
reasoning asks questions such as

107
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:04,060
does this proposal violate an
existing architectural principle?

108
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:08,470
Does this design satisfy the
required available characteristic?

109
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:12,150
The system is not simply looking
for relevant information.

110
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:15,080
It is evaluating one
piece of knowledge in the

111
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:20,440
context of another. Architectural
decisions frequently involve alternatives.

112
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:25,370
Comparative reasoning examines
options in the context of the

113
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:29,790
problem being solved. The
important comparison is rarely a

114
00:07:29,850 --> 00:07:32,770
generic list of advantages
and disadvantages.

115
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:36,580
An option may be appropriate
in one architectural context

116
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:41,560
and unsuitable in another. The
reasoning therefore needs to

117
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:45,300
consider the requirements,
characteristics, principles, and the

118
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:47,880
existing architecture
surrounding that decision.

119
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:53,300
Architecture evolves. A decision
that is correct today may

120
00:07:53,410 --> 00:07:56,470
replace an earlier decision
that was equally correct when

121
00:07:56,530 --> 00:08:03,770
it was made. Temporal reasoning
allows Articulate to understand

122
00:08:03,810 --> 00:08:07,480
that architectural knowledge
exists within time.

123
00:08:07,570 --> 00:08:11,240
It can help answer
questions such as what did

124
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:15,130
this decision replace? Why
did the architecture change?

125
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:19,690
Which assumptions were true
when this decision was made?

126
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:23,800
This becomes increasingly important
as the Knowledge Model evolves.

127
00:08:23,930 --> 00:08:27,060
Architectural knowledge should
not exist without context.

128
00:08:27,700 --> 00:08:33,700
Claims may be supported by conversations,
documents, observations, and

129
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:39,070
other evidence. Evidential
reasoning allows the system to ask

130
00:08:39,150 --> 00:08:43,240
what supports this conclusion,
where did this information come

131
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:46,070
from, and how confident
should we be in it?

132
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:50,600
This is particularly important for
an AI-native architecture system

133
00:08:50,730 --> 00:08:53,940
because not everything discovered
or inferred by AI should

134
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:56,960
ultimately become accepted
architectural truth.

135
00:08:57,130 --> 00:09:00,020
These are not necessarily five agents.

136
00:09:00,110 --> 00:09:04,990
That distinction matters. These
are reasoning capabilities.

137
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:08,740
How these capabilities are
eventually implemented is a separate

138
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:12,340
architectural decision. This
is where the idea of Agentic

139
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:17,080
RAG becomes useful. Instead
of following a fixed retrieval

140
00:09:17,210 --> 00:09:19,770
pipeline, an agent can decide
how knowledge should be

141
00:09:19,850 --> 00:09:22,650
gathered based on the question
it's trying to answer.

142
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:26,260
A reasoning process might
begin with semantic search to

143
00:09:26,330 --> 00:09:30,660
identify relevant concepts. It might
then traverse relationships within

144
00:09:30,890 --> 00:09:34,580
the Knowledge Model. It could
then use structured queries

145
00:09:34,660 --> 00:09:37,930
to retrieve specific
architectural information.

146
00:09:38,060 --> 00:09:41,450
The results may reveal that
additional knowledge is required,

147
00:09:41,490 --> 00:09:44,170
causing the reasoning process
to perform further retrieval.

148
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:49,080
The important difference
here is that retrieval is no

149
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:51,810
longer a single step before generation.

150
00:09:51,950 --> 00:09:55,100
Retrieval becomes part
of the reasoning process.

151
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:58,840
The system can decide what
information it needs, retrieve

152
00:09:58,920 --> 00:10:01,873
it, examine what it has
discovered, and determine whether

153
00:10:01,953 --> 00:10:05,130
further knowledge is
required. But I think even the

154
00:10:05,170 --> 00:10:09,800
term Agentic RAG risks making
retrieval the centre of

155
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:15,480
the architecture. For Articulate,
the more useful concept is

156
00:10:15,550 --> 00:10:20,430
a Knowledge Reasoning Layer. The
Knowledge Model is responsible

157
00:10:20,510 --> 00:10:24,600
for representing what Articulate
knows about the architecture.

158
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:30,650
The Knowledge Reasoning Layer is
responsible for helping Articulate

159
00:10:30,780 --> 00:10:35,470
reason with that knowledge.
Conceptually, it sits between reasoning

160
00:10:35,550 --> 00:10:39,160
capabilities and the underlying
ways in which knowledge can

161
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:42,270
be accessed. This is a
conceptual boundary rather than

162
00:10:42,370 --> 00:10:46,690
an implementation design. The
purpose is to separate two

163
00:10:46,770 --> 00:10:51,880
concerns. The Knowledge Model
represents architectural knowledge.

164
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:55,400
The reasoning layer determines
how that knowledge should be

165
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:58,540
explored and interpreted
for a particular problem.

166
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:02,290
This means an agent should
not necessarily need to

167
00:11:02,340 --> 00:11:05,190
understand how every piece
of knowledge is stored.

168
00:11:05,270 --> 00:11:08,660
Instead, it needs capabilities
that allow it to ask

169
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:11,380
meaningful questions
of the Knowledge Model.

170
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:15,860
The details of whether those
questions require semantic retrieval,

171
00:11:15,960 --> 00:11:20,850
graph traversal, structured queries,
or a combination of approaches

172
00:11:20,930 --> 00:11:23,710
belong within the
Knowledge Reasoning Layer.

173
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:27,720
One of the lessons I'm
beginning to take from

174
00:11:27,770 --> 00:11:31,690
designing Articulate is that
starting with agents may be

175
00:11:31,770 --> 00:11:33,790
the wrong level of abstraction.

176
00:11:33,850 --> 00:11:36,760
It's easy to identify a
problem and then immediately

177
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:38,180
create an agent for it.

178
00:11:38,250 --> 00:11:42,430
A research agent, a decision
agent, an impact analysis

179
00:11:42,470 --> 00:11:44,360
agent, and a principle agent.

180
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:47,250
But the existence of
an agent tells us very

181
00:11:47,330 --> 00:11:51,270
little about the reasoning
it actually needs to perform.

182
00:11:52,040 --> 00:11:55,620
Before designing an agent,
I think the more useful

183
00:11:55,700 --> 00:12:00,080
questions are, what problem
is it trying to solve?

184
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:02,920
What kind of reasoning
does that problem require?

185
00:12:03,090 --> 00:12:06,170
What knowledge does that
reasoning depend upon?

186
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:09,680
How should that knowledge be
retrieved and what evidence

187
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:12,810
should support the result?
Only then does it make

188
00:12:12,850 --> 00:12:16,840
sense to ask how the capability
should be implemented

189
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:19,740
and whether an agent is the
appropriate runtime abstraction.

190
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:24,380
This follows a wider architectural
approach I'm trying to

191
00:12:24,460 --> 00:12:28,130
take throughout this project. The
architecture should emerge from

192
00:12:28,210 --> 00:12:31,260
the problem and the capabilities
required to solve it.

193
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:32,800
The agents can come later.

194
00:12:33,210 --> 00:12:36,590
There is another idea that
becomes possible once knowledge

195
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:38,230
and reasoning are treated separately.

196
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:41,930
The Knowledge Model can tell
Articulate what it knows,

197
00:12:41,970 --> 00:12:45,090
but it does not necessarily
explain how a particular

198
00:12:45,170 --> 00:12:48,940
conclusion was reached. A reasoning
process may traverse these

199
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:53,360
relationships and conclude that
changing the messaging platform could

200
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:55,870
affect a specific business capability.

201
00:12:56,170 --> 00:12:59,650
The relationship exists in
the Knowledge Model, but the

202
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:01,930
path taken through those
relationships is part of the

203
00:13:02,010 --> 00:13:06,690
reasoning process. This suggests
a distinction between a knowledge

204
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:08,300
graph and a reasoning map.

205
00:13:08,580 --> 00:13:13,000
The knowledge graph represents the
connected architectural knowledge

206
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:16,250
available to the system. A
reasoning map represents the

207
00:13:16,290 --> 00:13:19,660
path taken through the knowledge
while answering a particular

208
00:13:19,740 --> 00:13:23,090
question. The reasoning map
is not necessarily part of

209
00:13:23,170 --> 00:13:26,530
the permanent architectural
knowledge. It is an artefact of

210
00:13:26,580 --> 00:13:28,840
reasoning, but it may become valuable.

211
00:13:29,050 --> 00:13:31,450
It provides a way to
inspect how a conclusion

212
00:13:31,750 --> 00:13:35,110
was reached. It can show
which knowledge was used

213
00:13:35,150 --> 00:13:37,340
and it can expose assumptions.

214
00:13:37,660 --> 00:13:41,270
It may allow an architect
to challenge a reasoning

215
00:13:41,350 --> 00:13:44,440
path rather than simply
accepting or rejecting the final

216
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:49,560
answer. This becomes particularly
important when AI is participating

217
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:53,290
in architectural decisions.
A useful answer is important.

218
00:13:54,090 --> 00:13:58,680
Understanding how the system
reached that answer may be

219
00:13:58,760 --> 00:14:02,400
just as important. The Knowledge
Model establishes the foundation

220
00:14:02,480 --> 00:14:05,610
for representing architecture
as connected knowledge.

221
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:08,780
Knowledge reasoning adds the next layer.

222
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:12,520
Articulate needs to do more
than retrieve information that

223
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:13,970
appears relevant to a question.

224
00:14:14,010 --> 00:14:15,730
It needs to understand relationships.

225
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:17,770
It needs to evaluate constraints.

226
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:22,680
It needs to compare alternatives
within the architectural context.

227
00:14:22,760 --> 00:14:25,560
It needs to reason
across time and it needs

228
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:28,870
to understand the evidence
behind what it knows.

229
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:31,880
And it needs to explain how it reached its

230
00:14:31,960 --> 00:14:37,840
conclusions. Semantic search, graph
traversal, and structured queries remain

231
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:41,480
useful. Agentic RAG provides
a useful model for allowing

232
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:45,580
agents to choose between those
retrieval strategies dynamically.

233
00:14:45,630 --> 00:14:48,930
But none of them individually
are Architectural Intelligence.

234
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:54,320
Architectural Intelligence emerges from
combining structured knowledge with

235
00:14:54,400 --> 00:14:58,960
explicit reasoning capabilities.
If Articulate is going to contain

236
00:14:59,040 --> 00:15:02,780
agents that perform these reasoning
capabilities, how should their

237
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:06,740
intended behaviour be described?
This is where DCL enters

238
00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:10,430
the architecture, a topic
for the next episode.

239
00:15:10,550 --> 00:15:15,400
If you're interested in AI-native
architecture, architectural knowledge, or

240
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:19,090
building systems that reason,
rather than simply retrieve, I

241
00:15:19,170 --> 00:15:21,570
hope you join me for the next episode.
